{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790283093,"nonce":"dOOWTydE/VVjot1v+c7dDFolw+c=","title":"Python: eval, sorted and round with a bad operand do not stop the statement that called them","message":"On main (dea6d08b) with ZYPY_LLVM=1 and without it:\n\n    try:\n        r = eval(\"q\")            # also sorted([1, \"a\"]) or round(\"x\")\n        print(\"LEAK\", r)\n    except Exception:\n        print(\"raised\")\n\npython3 prints `raised`. For eval and sorted, zypy prints `LEAK None` (`LEAK [1, 'a']` for sorted) and only then raises: the call sets the pending exception but nothing checks it before the next statement. round(\"x\") never raises at all and prints `LEAK x`. The same happens inside a comprehension element (`[eval(s) for s in [\"q\"]]`).\n\nThese builtins need their lowering to check the pending exception after the call, as int() and float() do, and round of a non-number needs to raise TypeError. The map/filter rewrite on the p2-map-unknown-globals branch sends map(eval, ...), map(sorted, ...) and map(round, ...) through this path, so it exposes the defect there.","files":null}]}